INT. PROBATE COURT - EMPTY CHAMBERS - LATE AFTERNOON

Dust turns in a shaft of amber light. A single leather chair stands where the judge once sat.

ATTORNEY RHONDA VASQUEZ-LEE (50s) shuts a thick folder and does not look up. Across the long oak table, her brother-in-law TEDDY "TEDS" LEE (50s) watches her hands.

He clears his throat. She ignores it.

TEDDY
Rhonda. Come on.

RHONDA
Don't "Rhonda, come on" me, Teds.

He leans back. She finally lifts her eyes. Hers are dry, but he is the one who looks away.

TEDDY
Dad wanted it split.

RHONDA
Dad wanted a lot of things.

He stands, paces to the window. She follows him with her gaze, then rises too, slowly, one hand on the table as if to steady it.

TEDDY
You're really going to contest this.

RHONDA
I'm going to read it aloud. Every word. Into the record.

He turns. She holds up the folder, and for a moment the light catches the red wax seal.

TEDDY
You wouldn't.

She opens it. He crosses the room in three strides, but she is already reading.
